{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>When one hunts any beast or bird, etc.</b> The essential reason for the covering is to create a mark of distinction, so that one will not come to eat blood. But with domestic animals there is no need for this mark of distinction, for he is already admonished and stands warned by what he sees — that the blood has been placed upon the altar, as it is written: ״וַאֲנִי נְתַתִּיו לָכֶם עַל הַמִּזְבֵּחַ לְכַפֵּר עַל נַפְשׁוֹתֵיכֶם״. And this is not to say that this is the essential reason for the prohibition of blood — for if so, why did He forbid the blood of beast and bird? Rather, the essential reason for the prohibition is that one should not eat the soul together with the flesh. And He gave a sign that the soul is included within the blood — for נְתַתִּיו עַל הַמִּזְבֵּחַ לְכַפֵּר עַל נַפְשׁוֹתֵיכֶם, and this atonement is surely soul in exchange for soul. And from this you may understand of your own intellect the reason for the prohibition of blood, which is on account of the soul that is in it. And this reason is equal for domestic animal, beast, and bird. But the covering is to create a mark of distinction so that one will not come to eat it. Yet for the blood of a domestic animal he needs no mark of distinction, for it suffices for him in this matter that he sees the blood has been placed upon the altar, and this is a sign for him that the blood is the soul. And furthermore, it is not fitting that the servant should eat from the table of his master. But for the blood of beast and bird there is no mark of distinction at all; therefore He commanded to create a mark of distinction in at least some respect through covering. And even though turtledoves and young pigeons are offered upon the altar, none the less they are nullified in their infrequency, for most birds are not for the altar as are domestic animals." "Another thing: since the flesh of beast and bird is not readily available, and they require hunting and come to a person through great effort or at high cost, and given that the thing is especially precious to him, there is reason to fear lest he eat the flesh together with the blood; therefore he must create a mark of distinction through covering. And our Sages of blessed memory (Ḥullin 84a) said: From here the Torah taught derekh eretz, that one should not eat meat except under this condition of preparation. And the reason for the matter you will find further on, in parashat Re'eh, at the verse ״כִּי יַרְחִיב ה׳ אֶת גְּבוּלְךָ וְאָמַרְתָּ אוֹכְלָה בָשָׂר״ etc. — see there."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {0 "The Kli Yakar quotes the phrase וַאֲנִי נְתַתִּיו לָכֶם עַל הַמִּזְבֵּחַ לְכַפֵּר עַל נַפְשׁוֹתֵיכֶם from Leviticus 17:11 without supplying the verse address; the citation is rendered as he gives it, inline.", 1 "The Talmud citation חולין פד is given in the Hebrew with page side unspecified; resolved to Ḥullin 84a based on standard printed editions where this teaching appears."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:37:10.697968345Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}